---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TokenTable from "../components/api/TokenTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "scales", label: "Second scale" },
  { id: "areas", label: "Areas" },
  { id: "cards", label: "Chart cards" },
  { id: "data", label: "Values and colors" },
  { id: "accessibility", label: "Accessibility" },
  { id: "javascript", label: "JavaScript" },
  { id: "tokens", label: "Tokens" },
]);

const half = ["Jan", "Feb", "Mar", "Apr", "May", "Jun"];
const months = [...half, "Jul", "Aug", "Sep", "Oct", "Nov", "Dec"];
const revenue = [62, 71, 68, 80, 76, 89];
const target = [65, 65, 70, 70, 75, 75];
const spend = [12, 15, 14, 18, 20, 22];
const conversions = [340, 410, 390, 480, 520, 610];
const traffic = [8.2, 9.4, 10.1, 9.8, 11.6, 12.4];
const bounce = [58, 55, 52, 54, 49, 46];
const tickets = [142, 158, 130, 175, 160, 190];
const hours = [5.2, 4.8, 5.5, 4.3, 4.6, 3.9];
const yearRevenue = [420, 460, 510, 480, 560, 610, 590, 640, 700, 680, 750, 820];
const margin = [18, 19, 21, 20, 22, 24, 23, 25, 27, 26, 28, 30];
const growth = [4, 5, 6, 5, 7, 8, 7, 9, 10, 9, 11, 13];
---
<DocsLayout page="mixed-chart">
<PageBody sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          A <code>&lt;figure class="mixed-chart"&gt;</code> draws a
          <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> as columns, lines and areas on one plot.
          Each series header's class picks how it is drawn.
        </p>
        <p>
          It takes the same markup as a <a href="line-chart.html">line
          chart</a>. Give a header cell <code>column</code> to draw that series
          as columns from zero, or <code>area</code> for a line with a fading
          fill under it. Any other series is a line, and
          <code>dashed</code> dashes it. Columns sit behind the lines. A legend
          names the series, and a tooltip follows the pointer or the arrow keys
          from row to row, with a point on each line.
        </p>
        <figure class="mixed-chart" data-markdown-ignore>
          <figcaption>Revenue against target, $K</figcaption>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Month</th><th class="column">Revenue</th><th class="dashed">Target</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{half.map((month, i) => <tr><th>{month}</th><td>${revenue[i]}</td><td>${target[i]}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </figure>
<Code code={`
<figure class="mixed-chart">
  <figcaption>Revenue against target, $K</figcaption>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Month</th><th class="column">Revenue</th><th class="dashed">Target</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Jan</th><td>$62</td><td>$65</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Feb</th><td>$71</td><td>$65</td></tr>
      <!-- … -->
    </tbody>
  </table>
</figure>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.scales}>
        <p>
          Series share one scale unless their header has <code>end</code>.
          Those are drawn against a second scale fitted to their own values,
          so a series in other units or of another size still fills the plot.
          Set <code>data-end-min</code> and <code>data-end-max</code> on the
          figure to fix that scale, as <code>data-min</code> and
          <code>data-max</code> fix the first. The chart draws no y axis, so
          name both units in the caption or the headers.
        </p>
        <figure class="mixed-chart" data-markdown-ignore>
          <figcaption>Ad spend ($K) and conversions</figcaption>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Month</th><th class="column">Spend</th><th class="end">Conversions</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{half.map((month, i) => <tr><th>{month}</th><td>${spend[i]}</td><td>{conversions[i]}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </figure>
<Code code={`
<figure class="mixed-chart">
  <figcaption>Ad spend ($K) and conversions</figcaption>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Month</th><th class="column">Spend</th><th class="end">Conversions</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Jan</th><td>$12</td><td>340</td></tr>
      <!-- … -->
    </tbody>
  </table>
</figure>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.areas}>
        <p>
          An <code>area</code> series fills down to the bottom of the plot.
          Here traffic has an area and the bounce rate is a dashed line on a
          second scale from 0% to 100%.
        </p>
        <figure class="mixed-chart" data-end-min="0" data-end-max="100" data-markdown-ignore>
          <figcaption>Traffic (thousands of visits) and bounce rate</figcaption>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Month</th><th class="area">Traffic</th><th class="dashed end">Bounce rate</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{half.map((month, i) => <tr><th>{month}</th><td>{traffic[i]}</td><td>{bounce[i]}%</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </figure>
<Code code={`
<figure class="mixed-chart" data-end-min="0" data-end-max="100">
  <figcaption>Traffic (thousands of visits) and bounce rate</figcaption>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Month</th><th class="area">Traffic</th><th class="dashed end">Bounce rate</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Jan</th><td>8.2</td><td>58%</td></tr>
      <!-- … -->
    </tbody>
  </table>
</figure>
`} />
        <p>
          All three kinds fit on one plot. Several series can share the second
          scale: here the margin and the growth rate are both percentages
          from 0 to 35, while revenue keeps the first scale.
        </p>
        <figure class="mixed-chart" data-end-min="0" data-end-max="35" data-markdown-ignore>
          <figcaption>Revenue ($K), profit margin and customer growth</figcaption>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Month</th><th class="column">Revenue</th><th class="area end">Profit margin</th><th class="dashed end">Customer growth</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{months.map((month, i) => <tr><th>{month}</th><td>${yearRevenue[i]}</td><td>{margin[i]}%</td><td>{growth[i]}%</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </figure>
<Code code={`
<figure class="mixed-chart" data-end-min="0" data-end-max="35">
  <figcaption>Revenue ($K), profit margin and customer growth</figcaption>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Month</th>
        <th class="column">Revenue</th>
        <th class="area end">Profit margin</th>
        <th class="dashed end">Customer growth</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>Jan</th><td>$420</td><td>18%</td><td>4%</td></tr>
      <!-- … -->
    </tbody>
  </table>
</figure>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.cards}>
        <p>
          A chart goes in a card with the figures it explains. The card adds
          no chart styles; set the plot's height with
          <code>--md-comp-mixed-chart-height</code>.
        </p>
        <article class="outlined" data-markdown-ignore>
          <dl class="stats my-0">
            <div><dt>Support tickets</dt><dd>190</dd><dd class="stat-change up">Resolved in 3.9h on average</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <figure class="mixed-chart" style="--md-comp-mixed-chart-height: 120px">
            <figcaption class="visually-hidden">Tickets and resolution time by month</figcaption>
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Month</th><th class="area">Tickets</th><th class="dashed end">Hours to resolve</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>{half.map((month, i) => <tr><th>{month}</th><td>{tickets[i]}</td><td>{hours[i]}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
            </table>
          </figure>
        </article>
<Code code={`
<article class="outlined">
  <dl class="stats my-0">
    <div>
      <dt>Support tickets</dt>
      <dd>190</dd>
      <dd class="stat-change up">Resolved in 3.9h on average</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
  <figure class="mixed-chart" style="--md-comp-mixed-chart-height: 120px">
    <figcaption class="visually-hidden">Tickets and resolution time by month</figcaption>
    <table><!-- … --></table>
  </figure>
</article>
`} />
        <p>
          <code>sparkline</code> is a 48px chart with no grid, labels, legend
          or tooltip, for a trend beside a figure that already explains it.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.data}>
        <p>
          Cells read as in a line chart: a cell's number is its
          <code>data-value</code>, or its text without currency signs, percent
          signs or grouping commas. The tooltip shows each cell's text as
          written. A scale with columns always includes zero. Otherwise it
          spans its values with a tenth to spare at each end, unless fixed by
          the data attributes or the <code>min</code> and <code>max</code>
          options. A gap breaks a line and leaves a column's slot empty.
        </p>
        <p>
          Series take <code>primary</code>, <code>tertiary</code>,
          <code>secondary</code> and <code>on-surface-variant</code> in
          column order, whatever their kind; a fifth series repeats the first
          color.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.accessibility}>
        <p>
          The table is the chart's text alternative. Give it a header row and
          a header cell per row, and name the chart with a
          <code>&lt;figcaption&gt;</code>. Since there is no y axis, put the
          units in the caption, the headers or the cells. The drawn marks,
          labels and legend are hidden from assistive technology.
        </p>
        <p>
          The plot is one Tab stop named by the caption. Left and right move
          between rows, Home and End jump to the ends and Escape hides the
          tooltip. In a right-to-left page the rows and the arrows run right
          to left. On a touch screen, tap a row to show it. The tooltip is a
          polite live region, so a screen reader hears each row's values as it
          moves. A sparkline is not focusable. Dashed lines stay apart from
          solid ones without color, and in forced colors the marks keep their
          colors so the legend still keys them.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.javascript}>
        <p>
          <code>AutoInit()</code> starts every <code>.mixed-chart</code>. To
          start one yourself, or to redraw a chart after changing its table,
          call <code>MixedChart.init</code> again. It has the line chart's
          methods and options; <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> apply to
          the first scale.
        </p>
<Code code={`
const chart = Expressive.MixedChart.init(document.querySelector('.mixed-chart'));
chart.show(2);   // highlight the third row; -1 hides the tooltip
chart.destroy(); // remove the drawing and show the table again
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.tokens}>
        <p>Override these on the <code>.mixed-chart</code>.</p>
        <TokenTable>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-mixed-chart-height</code></td><td>240px, the plot's height; 48px for a sparkline</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-mixed-chart-column-width</code></td><td>50% of each row's width</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-mixed-chart-shape</code></td><td>4px, a column's end radius</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-mixed-chart-line-width</code></td><td>3px; 2px for a sparkline</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-mixed-chart-grid-color</code></td><td><code>outline-variant</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--md-comp-mixed-chart-color-1</code> … <code>-4</code></td><td><code>primary</code>, <code>tertiary</code>, <code>secondary</code>, <code>on-surface-variant</code></td></tr>
        </TokenTable>
        <p>For a selective Sass build, include <code>"mixed-chart"</code> in <code>$components</code>.</p>
  </Section>
</PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
